HIKARU
------

Move the slave GPU access to hikaru-gpu.c. Proper support may require the
introduction of a new vk_device_accessors_t class.

MIE LLE (src/odin/mie.cpp, ODIN_MIE=lle): HACK in src/odin/mie_bridge.cpp,
port E bits 0-3 read high while the port is an output; the BOOTROM waits
for that at 0x0C00B860 and MAME reads 0xFF there. Measure the pins. The
LLE path is the default; drop the HLE stand-in in hikaru-mie.c once the
JVS inputs (players, coins, analog) are wired to the real board.
Ports 0x40/0x60/0x80/0x81, touched by the mask ROM init, are logged as
unknown. The serial adapter block (ports 0x09-0x0E) is modelled from
the Z80 code von4 uploads; its reply delays are HEURISTIC (mie.cpp
SERIAL_SEND_CYCLES, SERIAL_REQUEST_CYCLES), measure them on a board.
The CRP-1231 card reader on that line (src/odin/card_reader.cpp,
ODIN_CARD=none|reader) is an HLE stand-in: the LLE path is an H8/300H
core running the dumped firmware (MAME wangmid, crp1231lr10_ver0110.ic2).
Status byte st1 'V' that von4 tests on command 0x33 is not understood.

AICA (src/odin/aica.cpp + arm7.cpp): control block, timers, interrupts,
DMA, RTC and the ARM7 are in; still missing: the 64 slots and the DSP
(sound output), the real ARM7 speed (64 cycles per sample is MAME's
guess), the nested-FIQ question (does reading 0x2D00 drop the line?),
and the HACK in hikaru-aica.c (page offset 0 reads 4 for Pharrier).

ROM board 315-5881 (src/odin/crypt_315_5881.cpp, port of MAME, validated
on the real self-test of Planet Harriers and 19.7 M words against MAME's
code): RAM at local bus 0x0A000000, registers at 0x0A010000, keys from
MAME (airtrix, pharrier, swracer). Its ready bit is never busy (MAME).
The BOOTROM board probe answers a constant for the boards without the
chip (hikaru-memctl.c, HACK until those boards are measured).

GPU textures: the 0D1 UV offsets are added to the vertex UVs the way the
X360 port's vertex shader is assumed to (the Xenos microcode of vo3d was
not decoded: the sign is a HEURISTIC). Texhead filter mode 3 is trilinear
here while the X360 port leaves the sampler defaults (HEURISTIC). The
texel combine field of the 2C1 (bits 31:29) keeps the previous mode in
the port for the values 3, 6 and 7 and means plain texturing here
(HEURISTIC).
The mipmap levels of a texture alternate between the two texture banks,
as Valkyrie had it, where the X360 port keeps them in the texhead bank:
the pages BRAVEFF writes to the texture RAM itself hold the half-size
copy of each bank 0 texture in bank 1, and PODRACE keeps the textures of
the Watto shot of its intro where the level 1 of the same bank would
land (they stay intact on the cabinet).
The texture format 2 holds one 4-bit value a texel, a word being a block
of 2 x 2 texels (the BOOTROM font, 64 x 64 words for 128 x 128 texels);
the four channels get the value (HEURISTIC: the ramp of the PHARRIER
halo, 0 to F, is used for its alpha).
A texture transfer (IDMA) runs to its end whatever is written to its
control register, which reads busy until then (HEURISTIC, from the way
the library polls it; the boot ROM clears the register at a later
interrupt, in the middle of the next transfer of SGNASCAR).
The 2D layers cover the 3D where a pixel has its alpha bit (black
included: the bands of the Racer attract) and are transparent elsewhere
(its green 0x03E0 boxes) and layer 1 sits above layer 2, as the Racer
cabinet footage shows. The bit (bank - 1) of the unit register 3C turns
this keying on; without it the layer covers everything (the white SEGA
screen of von4 on the cabinet). Read from two cabinets only (HEURISTIC).
A layer whose bit of the unit register 38 is clear sits below the 3D,
which shows it where nothing was drawn when bit 0 of the 3C is set and
the clear colour has no alpha bit (the AIRTRIX logo and title screens).
Read from the cabinet footage and the registers of five games
(HEURISTIC).

GPU lighting follows the X360 port's vo3d shaders: the 691 colour is the
material ambient, the 091 colour stands for the texel of untextured
polygons, the 491 colour is the specular colour,
the light colour drives both the diffuse and the specular terms,
light vectors arrive in eye space (the library transforms them on the
SH-4), the 881 alpha bit turns
the vertex alpha into a blend factor between 091 and 291 (or the texel),
and the 451 carries the spot
cone (cos outer in 1.15, scale as an 8-bit float; the B61 vector aims it),
faded as sat ((L . axis - cos outer) * scale). The scale is the field
read as (1 + mantissa / 16) * 2^exponent (HEURISTIC: the library builds
it as an approximate reciprocal of cos inner - cos outer by subtracting
float bit patterns; with the exact reciprocal the hall-lit scenes of
AIRTRIX are 8 to 12 % darker than the cabinet, 2 to 5 % this way; the
wood itself is still 7 to 12 % under).
The back of a polygon drawn on both faces (vertex bit 23) is lit with the
reversed normal (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the SEGA logo of
PODRACE is as bright from behind as from the front and dark when its
back turns away from the light, which rules out the absolute value of
N . L; the inner faces of the PHARRIER letters). The back is told by the
winding: turning the normals toward the eye instead darkens the veils of
the PHARRIER galaxy, which the cabinet shows as bright as here. The X360
port emits the second face with the same normal.
Not as the port, read from the PHARRIER cabinet (HEURISTIC): the 491
colour is in 1/64 without the 8-bit wrap of the port (0x40 is 1.0), and
the specular lobe is the reflected ray against the view, raised to the
shininess byte, where the port takes the Blinn half vector (the SEGA
logo seen from the front stays blue, the canopy of the helicopter gets
its highlight).
Not as the port: the attenuation types 2 and 3 fall off on 1 / d and
1 / d^2 with the P and Q of the library (the port does not attenuate
them); the PHARRIER cabinet shows its asteroids untouched by the far
type 3 light of the logo scene.
A parallel light sent with a null direction and no attenuation lights
every side fully (HEURISTIC, read from the BRAVEFF cabinet, whose stages
add such a light, of colour 84 71 71, to every lightset: without it the
control room, the how to play rooms, the demo play and the wall of the
ranking are too dark). Weighing it by the material ambient (691), 0x7F in
the control room, leaves that room too dark whatever the capture curve:
the cabinet frame has more bright pixels than such a render.
The bits 19:16 of the 051 are the ambient level of the light, 0 to 15:
the light editor of PHARRIER shows them as "amb" next to the colour and
SGNASCAR builds them as (int) (16 * level). The light adds level / 16 of
its colour to every polygon it reaches, facing it or not, without the
material ambient, cut by the distance and by the cone like the Lambert
term (HEURISTIC for the scale and the two cuts, read from four cabinets:
with a level of 4 the track of SGNASCAR and its walls turned away from
the sun all gain 0.24 of their texture; the red beacon of BRAVEFF
flashes as strongly as on the cabinet, frame by frame; the board and
the disc of the AIRTRIX how to play; the explosion flash of PHARRIER.
Without the cut by the distance BRAVEFF and PHARRIER get far too
bright). von4 leaves the level at 0 and the X360 port ignores it.
Not as the port either, whose target clamps at 8 bits: a blended colour
is weighted by its alpha before it is clamped, so a lit colour above 1
keeps its excess (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the veils of the
PHARRIER galaxy burn to white and the white floor decals of AIRTRIX, at
an alpha of 0.7, look opaque).

EEPROMs: the main board 93C46 (SH-4 port A, src/odin/eeprom_bridge.cpp)
and the ROM board X76F100 (bus page 0x14, src/odin/x76f100.cpp) are
real and read by the BOOTROM; the MIE 93C46 is in the LLE MIE. It holds
the system settings of the BOOTROM (two copies of 62 bytes and a CRC,
the country in byte 5): ODIN_MIE_EEPROM=<file> loads it and writes it
back on exit. Blank, the BOOTROM falls back on Japan. The other two are
not written back to disk yet. The COUNTRY SETTING page of the BOOTROM
menu is the second routine of its EXIT line: how to reach it is not
found.

Game patches (hikaru.c, hikaru_install_game_patches) are all off, kept
as comments: airtrix 0x0C010F9A (660-frame I/O board wait shortened),
braveff 0x0C0407D0/0x0C0D522A/0x0C05B53E (forced results, purpose
unknown), pharrier 0x0C01C322 (AICA loop), sgnascar 0x0C00BC9A (EEPROM
byte) and 0x0C0130CE (600-frame I/O board wait). The BOOTROM EEPROM
patch (0x8E6) is off too. Remove the dead code once every game has been
seen running without it. Kept as a HACK: sgnascar 0x0C00BC9A, because
the X76F100 image is the Air Trix one (MAME has no NASCAR dump) and the
BOOTROM region check answers "ERROR 7" with it.

Bridge DMA (hikaru-memctl.c): the transfer completes at the start
write, like MAME. HEURISTIC: the real 315-6154 takes time, but games
refill the source buffer right after starting a transfer (von4 model
loader at 0x0C055FD4), so it must at least run ahead of the SH-4.


